<v Alex Blumberg>比洛杉矶更大的城市？

00:27:47.350 --> 00:28:11.730
<v Josh Bearman>All right. I guess more like a dense urban city, is what I meant to say. But so he doesn't have, like, a tool belt and show up and fix things. But he'll sort of call the guy who does that. And he takes the rent. And he is kind of a solitary guy, for the most part. He sort of likes to spend a lot of time reading and stuff. So he just kind of like hangs out up there in the building.
<v Josh Bearman>好吧。我想我是指人口更稠密的城市。所以他没有那种工具带，也不会出现修这修那。但他会叫人来做那些事。他负责收租。大部分情况下，他是个孤独的人。他喜欢花很多时间看书什么的。所以他只是在那栋楼里闲逛。

00:28:11.730 --> 00:28:26.610
<v Alex Blumberg>Josh and the rest of the tenants in the building like their super, whom we'll call Dave, and hung out with him. At the time of the story, Dave was going through chemotherapy for leukemia. And occasionally, he'd call different residents to ask for help with things. One Christmas, he contacted Josh.
<v Alex Blumberg>乔希和楼里的其他住户喜欢他们的管理员——我们叫他戴夫——并和他一起闲逛。在这个故事发生时，戴夫正在接受白血病的化疗。偶尔，他会给不同的住户打电话寻求帮助。有一年圣诞节，他联系了乔希。

00:28:26.610 --> 00:29:05.000
<v Josh Bearman>He calls and he says, this really weird thing happened. And I came over to his apartment. He had this orchid that was sitting on the table next to him. And he said, well, I had this visitor. And it was a very strange visit. And he started to tell me that a couple of months ago, in between chemotherapy treatments-- he'd had two chemotherapy treatments. And so he was out somewhere, and he was in his Land Cruiser, I think he has.
<v Josh Bearman>他打电话过来说，发生了一件非常奇怪的事。我去了他的公寓。他旁边桌子上放着一盆兰花。他说，好吧，我有了一位访客。那是一次非常奇怪的访问。他开始告诉我，几个月前，在化疗期间——他已经接受了两次化疗。所以他当时在外面某个地方，开着他的陆地巡洋舰，我想他有这么一辆车。

00:29:05.000 --> 00:29:37.130
<v Josh Bearman>And he was at this gas station, and he sees this woman pulls up in a big black Escalade. And she's on the opposite side of the pumps as him. She was an older woman, but he said very elegant and attractive in the elegant older woman way. And she had fancy clothes and, like, furs. And I think he mentioned that she had espadrilles on. And she was in this black Escalade, so she seemed to be classy or something, or well off.
<v Josh Bearman>他在那个加油站，看到一个女人开着一辆黑色大凯雷德停了下来。她在油泵的另一侧。她是一位年纪较大的女士，但他说是那种优雅且吸引人的年纪较大的女士。她穿着时髦，比如皮草。我记得他提到她穿了草编鞋。她开着这辆黑色的凯雷德，所以她看起来很有格调或者说很有钱。

00:29:37.130 --> 00:30:08.700
<v Josh Bearman>And so they're standing there at the pumps, and they start chatting. She initiates conversation with him, and they're getting on very well. And then he told me that she said something about him being bald, which was from the chemotherapy. And she said, are you bald from the chemotherapy? Or are you just bald because you think it's sexy? Something like that. And he was not offended by that. He thought it was kind of like this romantic repartee or something.
<v Josh Bearman>他们站在油泵旁，开始聊天。她主动找他搭话，他们聊得很投机。然后他告诉我，她说了点关于他秃头的事，那是化疗导致的。她说，你是化疗秃的吗？还是你觉得秃头很性感？诸如此类的。他并没有被冒犯。他觉得这是一种浪漫的应答。

00:30:08.700 --> 00:30:38.990
<v Josh Bearman>And then she gives him a card. And the card was sort of like an old style carte de visite that just had her name. It wasn't a business card that had a company or information on it. It was just sort of like a calling card that you used to leave at the door of Count Vronsky's estate when you would stop by or something. And so he remembered thinking that that was kind of a classy touch.
<v Josh Bearman>然后她递给他一张名片。那张名片就像那种旧式的访问卡，上面只有她的名字。它不是印有公司或信息的商业名片。它就像那种你以前去拜访沃伦斯基伯爵庄园时留下的名片。他记得认为那是一种有格调的举动。

00:30:38.990 --> 00:30:40.940
<v Alex Blumberg>And very mysterious.
<v Alex Blumberg>而且非常神秘。

00:30:40.940 --> 00:30:51.020
<v Josh Bearman>Very mysterious. And so then he gave her his information. And they parted ways.
<v Josh Bearman>非常神秘。然后他把信息给了她。他们分开了。

00:30:51.020 --> 00:31:07.140
<v Alex Blumberg>Until Christmas, two months after their first meeting at the gas station, when the mysterious lady called out of the blue. She'd asked how Dave was, and he told her about his recent health problems. An infection from the chemotherapy had turned serious, and he'd spent a couple of weeks in a hospital in a near coma.
<v Alex Blumberg>直到圣诞节，在他们在加油站初次见面两个月后，那位神秘女士突然打来了电话。她问戴夫怎么样了，他告诉了她最近的健康问题。化疗引发的感染变得严重，他在医院昏迷了近两周。

00:31:07.140 --> 00:31:20.550
<v Alex Blumberg>That's terrible, the lady said. Is there anything I can do? Can I come by and visit? Dave said yes, and so she'd arrived that morning dressed in the same elegant manner as at the gas station, bearing the orchid which Dave now had on his table. And they'd sat and chatted.
<v Alex Blumberg>太糟糕了，女士说。我能做点什么吗？我可以去看看你吗？戴夫说了好，所以她那天早上穿着和在加油站时一样优雅的方式到达了，带着戴夫现在桌上的那盆兰花。他们坐下来聊天。

00:31:20.550 --> 00:32:01.700
<v Josh Bearman>Somehow, the topic of fate came up. And that was the point when she said, I know this is kind of a weird time, but I actually have something I wanted to share with you. And he says, what's that? And she says that she was abroad with this group of investors who had developed this really interesting investment opportunity. And she says, I know that this is a weird time. But this is very exciting, so I feel like I should just let you in on this investment opportunity right now.
<v Josh Bearman>不知怎么的，谈到了命运这个话题。那是她开口说，我知道这可能不是时候，但我真的有些事想跟你分享。他说，什么事？她说她当时在国外，跟着一群开发了一个非常有趣的投资机会的投资者在一起。她说，我知道这不是时候。但这非常令人兴奋，我觉得我应该现在就让你参与这个投资机会。

00:32:01.700 --> 00:32:27.620
<v Josh Bearman>He was a little surprised. And but he said, oh, all right. So what's the investment opportunity? And she said, well, I've gotten involved with this very canny group of investors. And it seems that they have located this snowman that can bench press 400 pounds.
<v Josh Bearman>他有点惊讶。但他说了，哦，好吧。那是什么投资机会？她说，嗯，我加入了一群非常精明的投资者。似乎他们已经找到了这个可以卧推400磅的雪人。

00:32:27.620 --> 00:32:36.560
<v Josh Bearman>[LAUGHTER]
<v Josh Bearman>[笑声]

00:32:36.560 --> 00:32:38.890
<v Alex Blumberg>What?
<v Alex Blumberg>什么？

00:32:38.890 --> 00:32:53.540
<v Josh Bearman>Yeah, so-- I said-- so then I'm asking, OK-- well, what do you mean? What kind of a snowman?
<v Josh Bearman>是啊，所以——我说——那我问了，好吧——你是什么意思？什么样的雪人？

00:32:53.540 --> 00:32:57.130
<v Alex Blumberg>It's hard to exactly figure out what the proper follow-up question is.
<v Alex Blumberg>很难确切找出合适的跟进问题是什么。

00:32:57.130 --> 00:33:20.390
<v Josh Bearman>Yeah. And so I said, well, OK, so, like, it's the Abominable Snowman. He's like, no, no, no. She said it's a totally normal snowman with a carrot nose and the coal for the eyes and a top hat and everything. It's just that this snowman can bench press 400 pounds.
<v Josh Bearman>是啊。于是我说，好吧，所以，那是大脚怪吗？他像是说，不，不，不。她说那是完全正常的雪人，有胡萝卜鼻子、煤块眼睛、高礼帽什么的。只是这个雪人可以卧推400磅。

00:33:20.390 --> 00:33:50.510
<v Josh Bearman>And so then-- but so I'm saying, well, OK, but what-- what's the investment opportunity? Is he running a hedge fund or what? I mean, I don't understand. And then he said, no. See, what happened is the investors, they've got it all worked out. They're going to put together a variety show with the snowman as the lead act. And then they're going to take the snowman show on tour.
<v Josh Bearman>于是——但我说，好吧，但是什么——投资机会是什么？他在经营对冲基金还是什么？我是说，我不明白。然后他说，不。看，发生的是投资者们，他们已经全盘计划好了。他们要组织一场以雪人为主角的综艺节目。然后他们要带着雪人秀去巡回演出。

00:33:50.510 --> 00:34:30.410
<v Josh Bearman>And so the investment opportunity is the geographic territorial rights to the show. So when the show goes to different states, you get to reap the rewards if you've bought into the rights to the show. So the woman said that she had already-- she got in on the ground floor with this thing. And so she got California and Nevada, like the west. So he said that the United States was all snapped up. So but now Indonesia is wide open.
<v Josh Bearman>所以投资机会是节目的地理区域权利。所以当节目去到不同的州时，如果你买了节目的权利，你就可以获得回报。所以女士说她已经——她在这件事上抢占了先机。所以她拿下了加州和内华达州，就像西部地区。他说美国那边都已经抢光了。但现在印尼那边完全空着。

00:34:30.410 --> 00:34:35.890
<v Alex Blumberg>For the variety show starring the weightlifting snowman?
<v Alex Blumberg>为那个由举重雪人主演的综艺节目？

