<v Alex Blumberg>Right.
<v Alex Blumberg>对。

00:35:28.440 --> 00:36:01.370
<v Josh Bearman>They would have to really sort a lot of things out, just even the basic question of, well, how-- did the snowman roll himself up into three balls and find a carrot nose? And like, how did the investors find the snowman? That's the other thing that I was curious about. Were they driving around the woods and they just saw this snowman lifting logs, and they high-fived each other and said, all right, we've got a variety show? Like they're in a caravan of black suburbans with their cigars, like, we did it.
<v Josh Bearman>他们不得不梳理很多事情，哪怕只是基本问题，比如，嗯，怎么——雪人把自己滚成了三个球并找到了一个胡萝卜鼻子？而且，投资者是怎么发现雪人的？那是另一件我好奇的事。他们是不是开车穿过树林，刚好看到这个雪人在举木头，然后他们击掌说，好了，我们有个综艺节目了？就像他们开着黑色雪佛兰郊区车队，抽着雪茄说，我们做到了。

00:36:01.370 --> 00:36:16.130
<v Alex Blumberg>The weird thing was even though Dave found the story of a weightlifting snowman just as preposterous as Josh did, he seem somehow persuaded by this mysterious woman. He said it was hard to explain, but he felt like he'd known her his entire life. And he looked at life differently now, anyway.
<v Alex Blumberg>奇怪的是，尽管戴夫觉得那个举重雪人的故事和乔希觉得的一样荒谬，但他似乎被那位神秘女士说服了。他说很难解释，但他觉得他好像认识她一辈子了。无论如何，他现在对生活的看法不同了。

00:36:16.130 --> 00:36:51.550
<v Josh Bearman>He said something about how after-- when you get cancer or something like that, a serious illness, you just start to re-evaluate things. What might have seemed risky beforehand isn't now. And so he said, I kind of just felt like maybe there was a reason to take a risk. Maybe there's a reason I met the woman at the Shell station, and maybe this is kind of a risk to take. And then that's basically when he said, so I wrote her a check for $30,000.
<v Josh Bearman>他说了一些关于在——当你得了癌症或者类似的事情时，严重疾病，你只是开始重新评估事情。之前可能觉得冒险的事情现在不觉得了。所以他说，我只是觉得也许有一个理由去冒险。也许我遇到那个壳牌加油站的女士是有原因的，也许这就是一种要冒的险。基本上就在那时他说，所以我给她开了一张3万美元的支票。

00:36:51.550 --> 00:36:58.140
<v Alex Blumberg>Oh my God. What did you think?
