<v Ira Glass>Act Three, Please Re-Lease Me. That's re-lease me. We changed the names of the people in this story to protect the tenants involved.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕，《请重新出租我》。那是重新出租我。为了保护相关住户，我们改变了这个故事中人物的名字。

00:40:29.270 --> 00:40:47.430
<v Ira Glass>Sometimes somebody tells you your future, and you do not want to believe it. You can't believe it. When Dennis was 21, he became the super of this apartment building that his dad owned, a big 100-unit building in what was once a rough neighborhood. And his dad gave him this warning, father to son, super to super, about the tenants.
<v Ira Glass>有时候有人告诉你你的未来，而你不愿相信。你无法相信。丹尼斯21岁时，成了他父亲拥有的那栋公寓楼的管理员，那是一栋100单元的大楼，位于曾经的一个治安混乱的街区。他父亲给了他这个警告，父亲对儿子，管理员对管理员，关于住户的警告。

00:40:47.430 --> 00:41:20.590
<v Dennis>They will make a good person bad. Maybe you go into the landlording and business and you have good intentions and you're a good person. But people-- the lies they tell you, the tricks they play on you, the damage they do to your property, you will eventually lose that innocence and become a meaner person. I was like, wow. That kind of sucks. I hope that never happens to me.
<v Dennis>他们会让一个好人变坏。也许你进入房地产业做生意，你有良好的意图，你是个好人。但人们——他们告诉你的谎言，他们对你玩的把戏，他们对你财产造成的破坏，你最终会失去那份纯真，变得更刻薄。我说，哇。那真是太烂了。我希望那种事永远不会发生在我身上。

00:41:20.590 --> 00:41:45.560
<v Ira Glass>Dennis always figured he'd be a different kind of super than his dad anyway. His dad had been a plumber. Nothing made him happier than fixing the plumbing and the washing machines in the building. And his dad had some old world ways of doing things. If somebody was causing trouble, selling drugs or making a ruckus, he'd pound on doors and get in their face if he had to. The neighborhood was slowly improving, but when he first bought the building, people didn't care what the lease said or didn't say, and he had to deal with a lot of things man to man.
<v Ira Glass>丹尼斯总是认为无论如何他会成为一个与他父亲不同的管理员。他父亲以前是个水管工。没什么比修理楼里的水管和洗衣机让他更快乐的了。而且他父亲有一些老派的处事方法。如果有人在找麻烦，卖毒品或制造骚乱，他会敲门，必要时会冲到他们面前。街区在慢慢改善，但当他刚买下这栋楼时，人们根本不在乎租约写了什么或没写什么，他必须亲力亲为处理很多事情。

00:41:45.560 --> 00:42:05.150
<v Ira Glass>Dennis, meanwhile, was just out of college. His dad had scrimped and sacrificed to put him through Catholic schools, all the way from elementary up through his undergraduate degree. His mom-- his parents were split up-- his mom had always been a devout Catholic, communion every day. And all that stuff that the Jesuits taught Dennis in school about being a good person, he took it to heart.
<v Ira Glass>与此同时，丹尼斯刚从大学毕业。他父亲省吃俭用，送他上了天主教学校，从小学一直到本科毕业。他母亲——他父母分开了——他母亲一直是一位虔诚的天主教徒，每天做弥撒。耶稣会教士在学校教给丹尼斯关于做一个好人的所有东西，他都记在了心里。

00:42:05.150 --> 00:42:19.310
<v Dennis>One of the things that go into the Jesuit University is just like, they want you to be men and women for others. Not that everything I did, I tried to live up to that high standard, because I know I couldn't. But that was definitely in the back of my head.
<v Dennis>进入耶稣会大学的事情之一就是，他们希望你成为服务于他人的男人和女人。并不是说我做的每一件事都达到了那个高标准，因为我知道我做不到。但那绝对在我的脑海里。

00:42:19.310 --> 00:42:35.050
<v Ira Glass>Which brings us to our story. At first, the super job went great. People liked Dennis. He got Christmas cards from families in the building. But there was this one couple. They had lived in the building for almost 20 years, since back when his dad first bought the place.
<v Ira Glass>这就引出了我们的故事。起初，管理员的工作进行得很顺利。人们喜欢丹尼斯。他从楼里的家庭那里收到圣诞贺卡。但有一对夫妇。他们在那栋楼里住了近20年，从他父亲刚买下那个地方时就在了。

