00:42:44.460 --> 00:43:08.990
<v Dennis>Yeah, my dad liked them, yeah. He lived below them, actually, so he liked them. Literally lived right below them. My freshman year of high school, I lived with my father, so I lived below them, too, for a year. And so I saw them, and they knew who I was. I mean, even before that, even before high school, when we were little kids, we'd be running around, playing in the courtyard, hanging from the trees. But yeah, I mean, they were a regular fixture.
<v Dennis>是的，我父亲喜欢他们。他实际上住在他们下面，所以他喜欢他们。字面上就住在他们正下方。我高一那年，我和父亲住在一起，所以我一年也住在他们下面。所以我见到了他们，他们认识我。我是说，在那之前，甚至在高一之前，当我们还是小孩时，我们在庭院里跑来跑去玩，在树上挂着。但是的，我是说，他们是常规住户。

00:43:08.990 --> 00:43:25.970
<v Ira Glass>But everything for this couple changed when their daughter died. The woman quit her job to go to school. They threw all of their savings into that. But she had trouble with the classes, couldn't make the grade. And things just kept going downhill from there. She started drinking. They got behind on their rent.
<v Ira Glass>但这对夫妇的一切在他们女儿去世后改变了。那位女士辞职去上学。他们把所有的积蓄都投入了进去。但她在功课上有困难，没能达到分数线。从那以后情况就一直走下坡路。她开始喝酒。他们的房租迟交了。

00:43:25.970 --> 00:43:50.540
<v Dennis>She'd drop off a check to me. And then a day later or two days later, she'd call me and say, hey, did you deposit that check yet? And I was like, no, we haven't deposited it yet. Well, could you tear up that check and let me-- I'll give you another check. Forget about that check. And so then we'd tear up that check, and she'd bring down another check. And then that check would bounce. And I think they got to maybe like $4,000 in the hole.
<v Dennis>她会把支票给我。然后一两天后，她会打电话给我说，嘿，你把那张支票存了吗？我当时说，不，还没存。那你能撕掉那张支票让我——我会给你另一张支票。忘了那张支票吧。所以我们就撕掉那张支票，她又带了一张支票下来。然后那张支票也跳票了。我想他们可能亏了大约4000美元。

