<v Dennis>So I got her into bed. And she's in bed, and she's laying on her back. And I'm like, I've heard stories. Like, this is how Jimi Hendrix died or something like that. And I'm like, what if she pukes again? Is she going to choke on her puke?
<v Dennis>所以我把她扶上床。她躺在床上，平躺着。我想，我听过故事。比如，吉米·亨德里克斯就是这么死的之类的。我想，要是她又吐了怎么办？她会被自己的呕吐物呛死吗？

00:47:51.330 --> 00:48:01.620
<v Dennis>Finally I get a hold of her husband, and he's like, all right, I'll take care of it, I'll take care of it. But he's at work, and who knows when he's going to get home to take care of this? I'm down in the office, I'm trying to do work.
<v Dennis>最终我联系上了她丈夫，他说，好吧，我会处理的，我会处理的。但他还在上班，谁知道他什么时候回来处理这事？我在办公室里，试图干活。

00:48:01.620 --> 00:48:25.540
<v Dennis>And so I went back up and checked on her a couple of times. And she's sleeping, and she's breathing. And so then I go back down, and I can't get any work done because I was like, God, if this lady dies, I'm never going to forgive myself. I just remember that being a really terrifying experience.
<v Dennis>于是我回去看了她几次。她在睡觉，有呼吸。所以我回到楼下，没法干活，因为我想，上帝，如果这个女人死了，我永远不会原谅自己。我只记得那是一次非常恐怖的经历。

00:48:25.540 --> 00:48:38.020
<v Ira Glass>It's so weird because part of it is like, you have this business relationship with these people. But you're there in their building. Like, you're there in their house. Like, you own their house, you know? And so it's so personal.
<v Ira Glass>太奇怪了，因为部分原因就像是，你和这些人有这种商业关系。但你就在他们的楼里。就像，你就在他们的房子里。就像，你拥有他们的房子，你知道吗？所以这太私人了。

00:48:38.020 --> 00:48:58.210
<v Dennis>Yeah. Well, you definitely can't avoid it, so you've lived side by side with them. It becomes more of a neighbor-to-neighbor relationship.
<v Dennis>是的。嗯，你绝对无法避免，所以你和他们并肩生活。这变成了更多的邻里关系。

00:48:58.210 --> 00:49:22.180
<v Ira Glass>They were slipping on the rent. They'd plead with them. And it was always something. They had to pay other bills. Christmas or Thanksgiving coming up. They asked for extensions. And he was so inside their lives that at one point, he said, sure, they could pay a little later if he could see their tax forms to see what it is that they actually earn. And they showed him, $50,000 a year, which totally got under his skin.
<v Ira Glass>他们在租金上滑坡。他们哀求他。总是有些理由。他们要付别的账单。圣诞节或感恩节要到了。他们请求延期。他太深入他们的生活了，有一次他说，当然，如果能看看他们的税单来了解他们到底挣多少钱，他们可以晚点付。他们给他看了，一年5万美元，这完全让他感到反感。

00:49:22.180 --> 00:50:01.670
<v Dennis>I was like, you know what? Why can't they pay me? And I started basically putting pen to paper and running some numbers. And I say to the lady-- I'm like, look. Come down to the office. Bring your bills, and we'll do a budget. And I'll show you that you can afford to pay your rent. And after we literally picked every little nit picky thing there could be money that they could spend money on, like kitty litter, laundry, transportation to and from work for her husband, we had like $3,000 left over. And I was like, look, you can afford this apartment.
<v Dennis>我说，你知道吗？他们为什么不能付钱给我？我开始基本上拿笔在纸上计算数字。我对那位女士说——我说，听着。到办公室来。带上你的账单，我们来做个预算。我会让你看到你付得起房租。在我们逐一挑剔了所有他们可能花的钱之后，比如猫砂、洗衣、她丈夫的交通费，我们还剩下3000美元。我说，听着，你们付得起这个公寓。

00:50:01.670 --> 00:50:06.220
<v Ira Glass>So did that help? Did they end up paying the rent on time for the few months after that?
<v Ira Glass>那有帮助吗？他们在那之后的几个月里最终按时交房租了吗？

00:50:06.220 --> 00:50:08.080
<v Dennis>No, it didn't help.
<v Dennis>不，没用。

00:50:08.080 --> 00:50:09.350
<v Ira Glass>Wow, that's quite a lesson right there.
<v Ira Glass>哇，那真是个深刻的教训。

00:50:09.350 --> 00:50:27.610
<v Dennis>Yeah. It ended up getting really frustrating. And in the end, I was losing my patience, and I was getting really, really mad. She started running out of excuses. And she'd be like, well, my husband borrowed money from people at work. And he's got to pay them back. And that made me really mad.
<v Dennis>是的。结果变得非常令人沮丧。最后，我失去了耐心，我变得非常非常生气。她开始借口用光了。她说，嗯，我丈夫从同事那里借了钱。他必须还给他们。这让我非常生气。

00:50:27.610 --> 00:50:45.460
<v Dennis>I was like, look. You know what? You guys have been taking advantage of me. You've been playing me like a fiddle. And that's it. I'm not taking a back seat anymore. You guys get caught up. You start paying on time, or I'm going to kick you out. And so I was in July and August telling them, like, look. I don't care if it's Christmas. I don't care if it's Thanksgiving. I don't care if it's the holidays. I'm going to kick you out.
<v Dennis>我说，听着。你知道吗？你们一直在占我的便宜。你们一直把我当傻子玩。够了。我不再做退让了。你们得补上。你们得开始按时支付，否则我就要把你们赶出去。所以在7月和8月我告诉他们，看。我不在乎是不是圣诞节。我不在乎是不是感恩节。我不在乎是不是假期。我要把你们赶出去。

00:50:45.460 --> 00:50:58.150
<v Ira Glass>Can I ask what is going to be a really naive question? If you have a 100-unit building, like, in reality, how important is it that you get the rent on one unit?
<v Ira Glass>我能问一个很天真的问题吗？如果你有一栋100单元的楼，现实中，拿到一个单元的租金有多重要？

00:50:58.150 --> 00:51:22.230
<v Dennis>Well, I guess in the grand scheme of things, it's not going to make you or break you. But I mean, that's the deal. If you can't afford it, then I tell them, look, if you can't afford it, move to a smaller apartment. I'll give you a smaller apartment. But they didn't want to move. They did not want to move.
<v Dennis>嗯，我想从宏观角度来看，它不会让你成功或失败。但我是说，这就是交易。如果你负担不起，我就告诉他们，看，如果你负担不起，搬到一个更小的公寓去。我会给你一个更小的公寓。但他们不想搬。他们不想搬。

00:51:22.230 --> 00:51:38.350
<v Ira Glass>By the time Dennis was drawing up a budget for this couple, he'd been at it on and off for six years with them. And his feelings would vacillate all over the place. But his dad-- the very man who told him how running a building hardens you-- his dad never wavered in support of the couple.
<v Ira Glass>当丹尼斯在为这对夫妇制定预算时，他已经和他们断断续续打了六年交道了。他的感受会摇摆不定。但他父亲——那个告诉他管理大楼如何让人变硬心肠的同一个人——他父亲始终支持这对夫妇。

00:51:38.350 --> 00:51:49.300
<v Dennis>My dad and I would have battles. Like, we should kick them out. We should kick them out. My dad would be like, no, I don't want to kick them out. I'd be like, look, these people are way behind their rent. You gotta kick them out. Dad, just get rid of these people.
<v Dennis>我父亲和我会有争执。比如，我们应该把他们赶出去。我们应该把他们赶出去。我父亲会说，不，我不想把他们赶出去。我会说，看，这些人拖欠了这么多租金。你必须把他们赶出去。爸爸，摆脱这些人吧。

00:51:49.300 --> 00:52:23.710
<v Dennis>And we'd talk, and he'd reminisce about the stories. And one of the stories he tells me is that he went to the wake for the child that passed away. And he said to-- I think it was like the sister of the lady's husband. He's like, I used to hear the baby crying all night long, just crying and crying and crying. And the gentleman's sister is like, well, you're not going to have to hear the baby crying anymore. She turned around and walked away from him.
<v Dennis>我们交谈，他会追忆往事。他讲给我的故事之一是他去参加了那个去世孩子的葬礼。他对——我想是那位女士丈夫的姐妹说。他说，我以前整晚都能听到宝宝在哭，一直哭一直哭一直哭。那位男士的姐妹说，好吧，你再也不用听到宝宝哭了。她转身走开了。

00:52:23.710 --> 00:52:25.090
<v Ira Glass>Oh, she took it completely wrong.
<v Ira Glass>哦，她完全理解错了。

00:52:25.090 --> 00:53:01.610
<v Dennis>Yeah, my dad didn't mean it like, that kid was keeping me awake all night. Oh, thank God, now I can sleep. He's had kids of his own, and so he might know what it's like to see your kid in pain. So that, for him, was very, I think, just an emotional bond, where it was like, these people have just been through too much. I can't kick them out.
<v Dennis>是的，我父亲不是那个意思，比如，那个孩子整晚吵得我睡不着。哦，感谢上帝，现在我可以睡觉了。他自己有孩子，所以他可能知道看着自己的孩子痛苦是什么感觉。所以对我父亲来说，那是一次非常情感上的羁绊，就像，这些人经历得太多了。我不能把他们赶出去。

00:53:01.610 --> 00:53:15.230
<v Ira Glass>In the end, he decided to sit down one more time and have a heart-to-heart talk with the couple. He told them he knew that they were good people. They just had a problem and they needed help. He would wait for the money they owed if the woman would just get help and go to rehab.
<v Ira Glass>最终，他决定再坐下来进行一次交心谈话。他告诉他们他知道他们是好人。他们只是遇到了问题，需要帮助。如果那位女士愿意去康复中心接受治疗，他会等他们欠的钱。

00:53:15.230 --> 00:53:31.680
<v Dennis>If you do this, we'll repaint your apartment. Tell me what colors you want, and when you come back from rehab, we'll have this done for you. She wanted her kitchen painted yellow or something like that. And so she went to rehab. And I have a letter from her from August.
<v Dennis>如果你这样做，我们就重新粉刷你们的公寓。告诉我你们想要什么颜色，等你从康复中心回来，我们会为你做好这一切。她想要她的厨房刷成黄色什么的。于是她去了康复中心。我还有一封她八月份写给我的信。

00:53:31.680 --> 00:53:32.550
<v Ira Glass>OK, why don't you read it?
<v Ira Glass>好吧，你为什么不读一下？

00:53:32.550 --> 00:54:01.020
<v Dennis>Want me to read it? Sure. It says, well, I'm incarcerated and writing you from Cell Block C. You have no idea how touched I was by our talk, your caring, and generosity. The group here is very nice, as is the facility. I'm imagining what our apartment will look like upon my return. I'm so excited. I feel part of your family. You are in my prayers, which reminds me-- get your ass to church and make your ma happy.
<v Dennis>想让我读？当然。上面写着，嗯，我被禁锢了，正在从C区写信给你。你不知道我们的谈话，你的关心和慷慨是多么触动我。这里的小组很好，设施也很好。我正在想象我回来时我们的公寓会是什么样子。我太兴奋了。我觉得自己是你们家庭的一部分。你一直在我的祷告中，这让我想起——快点去教堂，让你妈高兴点。

00:54:01.020 --> 00:54:03.080
<v Ira Glass>And when you got this letter, did you feel good?
<v Ira Glass>当你收到这封信时，你感觉好吗？

00:54:03.080 --> 00:54:39.800
<v Dennis>Yeah, I felt really good. I was like, oh, this is great. She's in there, she's getting help. But then-- I don't know when it was-- a day or two or three later, I'm walking down the courtyard. And who do I see coming towards me but her. And this was supposed to be a two week or a month long stay. I was like, I can't believe it. Why is she-- and so that was the beginning of the end. Shortly after that is when I filed the court papers for eviction.
<v Dennis>是的，我感觉很好。我想，噢，太棒了。她在那里，正在得到帮助。但后来——我不记得是什么时候了——一两天或三天后，我在庭院里走着。谁迎面朝我走来？竟然是她。这本来应该是两周或一个月的停留。我想，我简直不敢相信。她为什么会——那成了结局的开始。没多久我就提交了驱逐诉讼。

00:54:39.800 --> 00:55:11.810
<v Dennis>It was just-- it was devastating. It was like, ugh, you have to stand in front of the judge, looking at this person who I ran up and down the courtyard and maybe they threw the ball to me when I was, like, eight years old. And now I'm sitting here in front of the judge, telling them, Your Honor, please remove these people forcibly from the building. I'm like, that's what it comes down to. I have to do what I have to do to win this case. I was mad. I wanted them out.
<v Dennis>这简直——太令人崩溃了。就像，呃，你必须站在法官面前，看着这个我曾经在庭院里跑来跑去，甚至可能在我8岁时还给我扔过球的人。现在我坐在这里在法官面前，告诉他们，法官大人，请强行把这些人从大楼里移走。我想，这就是结果。我必须做我必须做的事来赢得这场官司。我当时很生气。我想要他们出去。

00:55:11.810 --> 00:55:14.540
<v Ira Glass>And do you feel like this changed you?
<v Ira Glass>你觉得这改变了你吗？

00:55:14.540 --> 00:55:40.380
<v Dennis>Definitely. Yeah, definitely changed me. Now, I don't like to get personally involved with tenants. Like, it's just too hard. One time, these two guys moved into a two-bedroom apartment. And they were like, aw, you guys are great. Why don't you come out and have a beer with us? I was like, you know what? I got something to do tonight. I'm sorry. Maybe some other time, though. I'd really love to get a beer with you.
<v Dennis>肯定的。是的，肯定改变了我。现在，我不喜欢和住户有私交。这太难了。有一次，这两个人搬进了一个两居室。他们说，噢，你们真棒。为什么不出来跟我们喝杯啤酒呢？我说，你知道吗？我今晚有事。很抱歉。也许以后吧。我真的很想跟你们喝杯啤酒。

00:55:40.380 --> 00:56:09.360
<v Dennis>But I had no intentions of ever going out for a beer with them. I would just-- I don't know-- make up an excuse or avoid it at all costs because, you know, you can't be their friends. What I try and do now is I try to never have to kick somebody out. And the way I do that is by screening and screening and screening.
<v Dennis>但我从没打算过真的去跟他们喝啤酒。我只是——我不知道——编个借口或不惜一切代价避免它，因为，你知道，你不能和他们做朋友。我现在尽量尝试的是，我尽量不去赶任何人走。我做到这一点的方法就是筛选、筛选、再筛选。

00:56:09.360 --> 00:56:21.810
<v Ira Glass>But that's the thing that I find interesting about this. I never thought about that as like it has anything to do with your feelings. This goes beyond the business stuff, that you're checking out all their credentials and all that sort of stuff. Are you saying it's actually like, it's too emotional?
<v Ira Glass>但这就是我觉得这个故事有趣的地方。我从来没想过那与你的感受有关。这超越了生意，你检查他们所有的资历之类的东西。你是说这太情绪化了吗？

00:56:21.810 --> 00:56:46.720
<v Dennis>Yeah, it really is. I mean, for example, I do remember somebody coming in and applying for an apartment. And they're like, look, my credit's all messed up. I'm going through a divorce. And I don't know if my wife messed up my credit. And I was like, I don't care. Maybe that's the truth. Maybe that guy would have been a fine tenant. Maybe he would have paid his rent on time. But I just can't take that chance anymore.
<v Dennis>是的，确实是。我的意思是，比如，我记得有人来申请公寓。他们说，看，我的信用全毁了。我正在离婚。我不知道是不是我妻子毁了我的信用。我说，我不在乎。也许那是真的。也许那个人会是一个很好的租户。也许他会按时交房租。但我不能再冒险了。

00:56:46.720 --> 00:56:53.250
<v Ira Glass>What would your Jesuit teacher say about that?
<v Ira Glass>你的耶稣会老师会怎么评价这一点？

00:56:53.250 --> 00:56:55.860
<v Dennis>I don't know. Maybe they would be disappointed in it.
<v Dennis>我不知道。也许他们会失望的。

00:56:55.860 --> 00:57:00.680
<v Ira Glass>And do you care?
<v Ira Glass>你关心吗？

00:57:00.680 --> 00:57:18.470
<v Dennis>No. If they're mad about it, I would say, well, the next person who rented that apartment, Father, I did try to treat them justly and fairly and ethically.
<v Dennis>不。如果他们对此生气，我会说，神父，租下那个公寓下一个人，我是尝试公正、公平且道德地对待他们的。

00:57:18.470 --> 00:57:32.330
<v Dennis>[MUSIC - "WORKING ON THE BUILDING" BY ELVIS PRESLEY]
<v Dennis>[音乐 - 埃尔维斯·普雷斯利的“在建筑上工作”]

00:57:32.330 --> 00:57:57.400
<v Ira Glass>Well, our program was produced today by Alex Blumberg and myself, with Diane Cook, Jane Marie, Sarah Koenig, Lisa Pollak, and Nancy Updike. Our senior producer for today's show was Julie Snyder. Additional production on the rerun of today's show from Jessica Lussenhop, Katherine Rae Mondo, Stowe Nelson, and Matt Tierney. Peter Roach, the super there that you heard at the very beginning of the show, he retired a few years ago. He now performs in, quote, "every nursing home in Northeast Pennsylvania under the name, 'The Singin' Super.'"
<v Ira Glass>好吧，我们的节目今天由亚历克斯·布隆伯格和我自己制作，还有黛安·库克、简·玛丽、莎拉·柯尼格、丽莎·波拉克和南希·厄普代克。今天节目的高级制作人是朱莉·斯奈德。今天节目重播的额外制作由杰西卡·卢森霍普、凯瑟琳·雷·蒙多、斯托·尼尔森和马特·蒂尔尼完成。彼得·罗奇，那个你在节目开头听到的管理员，他几年前退休了。他现在以“歌唱管理员”的名字在东北宾夕法尼亚州的每一家疗养院表演。

00:57:57.400 --> 00:58:13.270
<v Ira Glass>Our website, thisamericanlife.org. This American Life is delivered to public radio stations by PRX, the Public Radio Exchange. Thanks, as always, to our program's co-founder, Mr. Torey Malatia, who has this message for any This American Life employee who's listening to the radio right now.
<v Ira Glass>我们的网站，thisamericanlife.org。《美国生活》由PRX（公共广播交流）发送给公共广播电台。像往常一样，感谢我们节目的联合创始人托里·马拉蒂亚先生，他给所有正在听收音机的《美国生活》员工带来了这个信息。

00:58:13.270 --> 00:58:18.730
<v Jack Hitt>Don't try to steal one of the light bulbs in the hallway. I know what you're thinking, but I've booby trapped them.
<v Jack Hitt>别想偷走走廊里的灯泡。我知道你在想什么，但我已经设了陷阱。

00:58:18.730 --> 00:58:23.730
<v Ira Glass>I'm Ira Glass. Back next week with more stories of This American Life.
<v Ira Glass>我是艾拉·格拉斯。下周带回更多《美国生活》的故事。
